    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d32-a3">

                <pb facs="0257" n="230" xml:id="pg230"/>


                <head>Paper Prepared in the National Security Council Staff<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d32-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian">undated</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">POINTS TO BE MADE ON START</hi></p>
                <list>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>We have a tough agenda for this meeting, but it is essential that we come
                        to grips with the hard issues before our negotiator heads back to
                        Geneva.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>Today, I would like to critically examine some of the most important
                        policy issues on which we face decisions, without getting mired down in very
                        technical details.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>These may be some of the most important decisions we make during this
                        Administration. They could change the way we think about strategic forces
                        for years to come. They will affect our deterrent capability. They certainly
                        will influence our support in Congress.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>Given our own plans to deploy two mobile missiles over the next several
                        years, we need to examine whether to change the current U.S. negotiating
                        position to ban mobile missiles.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>I also want to hear your views about how to approach the related issue of
                        limits on so-called nondeployed missiles.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>Finally, I want to review the status of air-launched cruise missile
                        limits.</item>
                    <label>—</label>
                    <item>If there are significant changes to be made, this may be the best time. On
                        the other hand, I am not interested in something that just grabs the
                        headlines for a few days, especially if all we get is a loud “nyet” from
                            Gorbachev that sets back the
                        negotiations.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
